sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch05-17> delphi:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/wolfram2002-ch05-17--grok> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/wolfram2002-ch05-17--grok> a delphi:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Grok 4.7 on wolfram2002-ch05-17: not-empirically-resolvable, resolvability 0.21" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0> ;
    delphi:assessmentType "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0" ;
    delphi:hasScore "0.214"^^xsd:double ;
    delphi:hasVerdict "not-empirically-resolvable" ;
    dct:type "resolution-route/none" ;
    delphi:hasConfidence "0.66"^^xsd:double ;
    dct:description "The text says there is no direct procedure because the system is given by constraints, which is the definition of that setup rather than a further fact. Nearby theorems about tiling being undecidable are stronger than anything this paragraph states, and the same section says that with tricks the examples at hand are feasible. No measurement distinguishes the sentence from the definition it restates." ;
    rdfs:comment "Proposed test with existing data: A constraint system is defined by allowed local templates rather than by an update rule, so the absence of a built-in evolution step restates that definition. Enormous effort has no cutoff that would separate this contrast from ordinary search." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-grok-4.7-high-fast> ;
    delphi:evidenceAgainst "The sentence contrasts a constraint with an explicit update rule, which is how the two setups are defined.", "Enormous computational effort names no complexity bound and no class of instances." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/wolfram2002-ch05-17--grok--operationalizability> a delphi:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Operationalizability of wolfram2002-ch05-17 by Grok 4.7: 0.28" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0-operationalizability> ;
